  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "If $T={x,{x}^{2},{x}^{3},y,{x,{x}^{2}},{x,y,y}}$, then $|T|=$",
  "worked_solution": "The cardinality of a finite set $T$ is the number of elements in $T.$ \n\nLet's start by listing all the elements of $T:$\n\n$$\nx,\\,{x}^{2},\\,{x}^{3},\\,y,\\,{x,{x}^{2}},\\,{x,y,y}\n$$\n\nNote that ${x,{x}^{2}}$ and ${x,y,y}$ each count as single elements of $T.$\n\nSince $T$ has $6$ elements, the cardinality is $|T|=6.$",
